Considerable effort has been devoted to designing additive noise mechanisms that achieve $(\epsilon,\delta)$-differential privacy for queries with $\ell_2$ sensitivity $\Delta_2$ while minimizing the resulting error. Let $\mathcal{V}_M(\Delta_2)$ denote the infimum of the mean squared error (MSE) over all $M$-dimensional additive noise distributions that are $(\epsilon,\delta)$-differentially private for every such query. We establish converse and achievability bounds on this optimal MSE. The converse (impossibility) shows that no feasible additive noise can achieve an MSE below $A_M(\Delta_2)$ by more than $\mathcal{O}(\Delta_2^2M^{-2})$, while under a further condition on the noise shape, the achievability (possibility) shows that generalized gamma noise achieves an MSE of at most $A_M(\Delta_2)+\mathcal{O}(\Delta_2^2M^{-2})$, where
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
        A_M(\Delta_2)=M\Delta_2^2v_0-\Delta_2^2C(\epsilon,\delta)+\frac{\Delta_2^2J(\epsilon,\delta)}{M}.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Here, $\Delta_2^2v_0$ is the exactly calibrated Gaussian variance per coordinate, and $C(\epsilon,\delta)$ and $J(\epsilon,\delta)$ are explicit functions of the privacy parameters alone. We derive the converse through the duality of error minimization under privacy constraints. To establish achievability, we develop an efficient noise calibration procedure that attains this upper bound. Our procedure determines the two shape parameters of the generalized gamma noise in closed form, avoiding the numerical shape search used by existing approaches. Relaxing the mild assumption on shape parameters required by our construction leaves the converse unchanged and increases the achievable upper bound by only $\mathcal{O}(\Delta_2^2M^{-1})$. Numerical evaluations support the theoretical results.

Can a single proper multiclass forecaster attain the minimax-optimal worst-case pseudo-U-calibration rate over all bounded proper losses? Can it do so while retaining the optimal logarithmic dependence on $T$ for every bounded Euclidean-smooth proper loss? Recent work gave the first simultaneous guarantee, but its bounds are roughly $K^{5/4}\\sqrt{T}$ in the worst case and include an additional $\\beta\\sqrt{K}\\log K$ term for smooth losses. We show that both dimension losses are unnecessary. A one-line, horizon-free Dirichlet forecaster achieves the optimal rates. After observing class counts $c_{t-1}$, draw the next prediction from $\\operatorname{Dir}(c_{t-1})$, on the face of classes seen so far. This is a fresh Bayesian bootstrap of the outcomes. The analysis rests on an exact identity: averaging any bounded proper loss under $\\operatorname{Dir}(\\alpha)$ equals a discrete derivative of its Dirichlet-averaged Bayes risk. The identity makes the be-the-perturbed-leader term telescope to a nonpositive Jensen gap. A one-count likelihood ratio then bounds stability by the inverse square root of that class's count. The resulting single, horizon-free algorithm satisfies

$$\\sup_{\\ell}\\operatorname{Reg}_{\\ell}
\\leq 4\\sqrt{S_TT}
\\leq 4\\sqrt{\\min\\{K,T\\}T}.$$

For every bounded $\\beta$-smooth proper loss $\\ell$,

$$\\operatorname{Reg}_{\\ell}
\\leq \\frac{5}{2}\\beta(1+\\log T).$$

Here $S_T$ is the number of observed classes. A class-aggregation reduction shows that the dimension-capped rate $\\sqrt{\\min\\{K,T\\}T}$ is minimax-optimal up to constants in every regime with $T\\geq24$, and known smooth-loss lower bounds match the logarithmic dependence on $T$ of the second bound. The proof covers nondifferentiable losses and changes of the active simplex face.

TL;DR: A Dirichlet forecaster simultaneously achieves minimax-optimal multiclass pseudo-U-calibration over all bounded proper losses and logarithmic regret for every Euclidean-smooth proper loss.
